WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.030
JIM: I first met our next
speaker and his lovely wife

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:07.110
Penny when I went out to
Claremont many years ago

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.280
to graduate school.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.910
He was the president at the
time of the Claremont Institute.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.460
And what I quickly learned about
him is that he's a teacher.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.060
It's something that Hillsdale
students learn about him today.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:21.720
He's a teacher.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.580
He's always teaching.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:27.180
He didn't teach formal classes
back then, as he does now.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.830
But we graduate
students certainly

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.020
learned a lot from
him, and he always

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.280
took the time to talk to
us when we had questions.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.190
Back in those days, when we
were younger and sillier,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.860
we used to kid him that he was
a college professor trapped

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.290
in a think tank
president's body.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:45.970
Now I guess he's a
college professor

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.820
in a college president's body.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.580
Well, if you come to see
us on the Hillsdale campus,

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.370
and we hope you do, you'll see
that he hasn't changed a bit.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:56.950
He's still teaching, and he's
now offering formal classes

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.230
on a variety of subjects.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.170
Those subjects include
Aristotle, the Constitution

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:03.250
and CS Lewis.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:07.270
And just last fall he taught a
course on totalitarian novels.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:09.940
Some of you may have seen his
essay in the December 2020

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.850
issue of Imprimis, where he
discussed one of those novels,

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.838
George Orwell's 1984.

00:01:15.838 --> 00:01:17.380
Well, he's still
pushing his students

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:20.300
to defend their opinions,
as he did with us,

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.220
and think them through
more clearly and deeply

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.400
because the goal of an
education worthy of the name

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.320
is discovering the
truth about things.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.213
And that's not easy.

00:01:32.213 --> 00:01:33.880
It's been my privilege
and honor to work

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:35.800
with him at Hillsdale,
my alma mater,

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:38.567
for now more than 20 years.

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:40.150
Please welcome him
back to the podium,

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.726
the 12th president of Hillsdale
College, Larry P. Arnn.

00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:47.221
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:55.195 --> 00:01:56.320
LARRY ARNN: Thank you, Jim.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:59.170
How are you?

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:02.720
So I want to begin with
two personal things.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.015
One is show the picture.

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.720
Now we're not behind in
our capital campaign,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:19.660
but if we were I would auction
that baby off right now,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:22.570
except my wife sits back there
and she told me I mustn't.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.060
That's Charlotte
Theresa, and everybody

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.990
thinks she's just
like her grandfather.

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.395
Thank you.

00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:31.270
That's enough of her.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:34.203
Nobody wants to look at me.

00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:36.370
I want to tell you some
stories about Rush Limbaugh.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.230
Because I've led a weird
life, for different reasons,

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.970
I've known Mark Levin and
Hugh Hewitt since a long time

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:53.680
before they got into radio.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.870
And I've met Rush Limbaugh
for the first time

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:59.410
in 1984, the year his
career was just taking off.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:01.903
He was in Sacramento,
California,

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:03.070
and I had business up there.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:05.800
I'd go, and the cab drivers
were all listening to him

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.720
and talk about how great he was.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.790
And then I ran across
him in a hotel,

00:03:09.790 --> 00:03:11.680
and I walked up
and shook his hand.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.165
And I said, you're dynamite.

00:03:13.165 --> 00:03:15.310
And he said, well, yes I am.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.190
I knew him off and on.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:25.370
Well, I've known him--
that's 36 years ago.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:29.120
He would come and
speak for things

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:32.420
that I was hosting at the
Claremont Institute and here.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:35.780
And I found out about him,
so I know him personally

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:36.350
pretty well.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.630
He's shy.

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.040
Can you believe that?

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:41.390
He's very shy.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:45.260
And he once came to Washington,
DC to speak at a dinner

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.870
where Newt Gingrich
was speaking.

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:51.320
And he asked us to drape off
a little area behind the stage

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.820
where he could drink Diet Coke
and wait to give his speech.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.710
And he sat back.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.430
There and they were,
like, 600 people there.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.180
And I walked back
there, and I said, Rush,

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.360
there's 600 people out there
would just love to see you.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:07.580
Why don't you come
have some dinner?

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.040
He said, no, I'm
drinking my Diet Coke.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.580
And he gave a speech, and
he walked back in that room,

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.370
and he went to his
airplane, and he went home.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:22.820
And next time I saw him, I
said, you're a really weird guy.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:24.830
And he said, I am, am I not?

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.960
He got to talking
about the college.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.160
And this was when?

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.710
This was 12 years ago.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.985
And something about
he liked the college

00:04:37.985 --> 00:04:39.110
and he knew a lot about it.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.150
But it struck his fancy.

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:43.002
And we got this huge response.

00:04:43.002 --> 00:04:44.960
Every time he'd mention
something on the radio,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.220
the phones would get jammed up.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:52.400
And then he called
one day, and he said,

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.240
I want you to
advertise on my show.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.700
And he had a big
wait list of people

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.123
to advertise on his show,
and I happened to know that.

00:05:01.123 --> 00:05:02.540
So I knew it was
kind of an honor,

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.880
but I thought it
was an odd idea.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.600
Let's advertise a college
on the Rush Limbaugh Show.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.040
And I said, OK,
I'll think about it.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:14.570
And then he sent Craig Kitchin,
who's his number one producer--

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:16.580
will be grieving today--

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.340
out to Hillsdale twice.

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.010
And both times he
sat all afternoon

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.490
through an Aristotle class.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:26.570
I was teaching it.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.112
He said, I want to get a sense
of what this college is about.

00:05:29.112 --> 00:05:31.010
And I said, OK
well, come with me.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:34.700
And I thought that was a
remarkable demonstration

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:37.760
of patience on the man.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.230
And I didn't do
anything about it.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:45.800
And then in this first week that
Obama was elected president,

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.540
he said to a bunch
of people, you've

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.350
got to stop listening
to Rush Limbaugh.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.810
And so I came to the Monday
morning staff meeting,

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:57.590
and I said, we're going
to examine this week

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.110
whether to advertise
on Rush Limbaugh.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.090
And John Sweeney
back there said,

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.820
so the president made
you mad, didn't he?

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:08.120
And I said yeah, because
that's the specific thing

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:11.690
that the president of United
States is supposed to protect,

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.750
us listening to and saying
what we want to, right?

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:16.640
It's our rights,
and he's an agent

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.140
of those rights, except not.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.270
And that did tick
me off, and that's

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:25.078
why we decided to do that.

00:06:25.078 --> 00:06:26.495
And then, of course,
it went wild.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:32.530
Over the years I would
go see him once a year

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.920
down in Palm Beach.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.450
He lived right on the
coast in Palm Beach.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:40.070
But if you've ever
been there, you

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.370
know it's a very precious place.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:46.510
And they wouldn't let him do his
radio program out of his house.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:48.520
Violated the zoning.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.980
So about five blocks away, sort
of in the more office area,

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.810
he bought a four story
building and kept it empty.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:00.850
He was the only one in it.

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:05.830
And you'd go see him, and
you'd arrive always on Friday

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:09.670
as he was finishing his
show, 3:30, I think.

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.040
And you'd go up.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:13.678
There weren't very
many people there.

00:07:13.678 --> 00:07:15.970
There were, like, three people
helping him do the show.

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:18.130
And Craig Kitchin
was always there.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:22.030
And we would sit and talk for
an hour, and it was just a hoot.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:27.800
And he would always make
fun of me the next Monday.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:33.180
So one day he said-- the
best conversation-- he said,

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:35.690
what do you want me to
say about the college?

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:38.580
And I said, I want
you to say it's hard.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:39.808
And he said, hard?

00:07:39.808 --> 00:07:41.600
And he looked at Craig
Kitchin and he said,

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.150
you know, we've been
in radio for 40 years.

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:47.270
Anybody ever ask us
to say that before?

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:48.610
I said, yeah, say it's hard.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.180
And he said, how hard is it?

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:51.920
And I said, well, it's hard.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.130
He said, how many people
get above a four point?

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.210
And I said, we don't do that.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:07:59.990
And he said, how many
get a four point?

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:03.110
And I said at that
time it was seven.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:04.183
He said, seven?

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:04.850
That's not many.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.100
I said, Rush, seven in 15 years.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.800
You know, we've got
great inflation now.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.710
That's up to like 20.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:19.560
And we had two in one year the
other day, a couple of years

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:20.060
ago.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:24.180
We all felt ashamed
of ourselves.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:28.070
And he said, well, why
don't they get a four point?

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:29.930
Is it chemistry?

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:31.640
And I said, yeah,
chemistry is hard.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.470
Everybody has to take that.

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.210
Physics, biology, math.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.159
Everybody has to take all that.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:39.770
I said, probably the hardest
department to get an A in

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:44.370
is the English department,
and if not that, politics.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.970
He said, English and
politics are hard?

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.450
I said, well, Rush, if you
want to understand them,

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.578
we don't give out many A's.

00:08:54.578 --> 00:08:56.370
The average kid coming
to Hillsdale College

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:00.690
has a 3.92 from high school,
and the average freshman

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.570
gets a 2.87.

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:07.290
We call that chart going
across the years shock and awe.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:12.910
And we recruit by
frightening them to death.

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:15.910
We show them that chart in the
recruiting because they're all

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.620
used to making A's.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:20.260
And they've got to lose that.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:25.480
They've got to not identify
their self-esteem with making

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:26.410
A's.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:30.160
And we always have a
bunch that make 3.94.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.030
There's a lot of them.

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:35.110
But we don't like them getting
all A's all the way through.

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:41.020
And he said, tell me how
you make the Constitution

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:42.460
course hard.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.860
And I said, Rush, the
Constitution of the United

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.200
States is, according
to James Madison,

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.710
a picture of the
human soul, also

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:53.350
a picture of the
order of nature.

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:55.840
And he starts out,
when he talks about it

00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:02.110
early in The Federalist
Papers, it's like a mechanism.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.070
Cancel this out so nobody
gets to nominate and pursue

00:10:06.070 --> 00:10:07.570
their own interest.

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:11.980
But it changes as it goes, and
it becomes simply beautiful.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:16.420
And I quoted those passages
to him in the 49th and 51st

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.770
Federalist.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:21.850
So then the next
Monday, he's supposed

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:24.940
to talk about us for,
I don't know how long,

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.040
a minute or something.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.150
And he very often went
five minutes, six minutes,

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.100
very long time in radio.

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.460
This next Monday
he went 20 minutes.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:39.500
And he just told all
about that conversation.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:43.870
And he said at the end, he said,
for the first time in my life,

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:46.510
I wish I had gone to college.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.900
And I'm going to go visit
Hillsdale College, which

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:51.910
he never did.

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.830
But on the other
hand, we were forever.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.830
I mean how many times,
has our call center

00:10:56.830 --> 00:11:00.580
answered the question, when
is Rush Limbaugh coming?

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:06.460
So he was a very bright.

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:09.880
He said to me once, he said,
how do you manage a college?

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:12.490
And I have some opinions
about how you manage things,

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:15.250
mostly from Churchill.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.650
The safeguard and
the glory of mankind

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:21.615
is that it is easy to
lead and hard to drive.

00:11:21.615 --> 00:11:22.990
So you don't want
to hire anybody

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:24.617
to do anything they
don't want to do.

00:11:24.617 --> 00:11:26.200
And you don't want
to hire anybody who

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.330
doesn't understand the goals.

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:30.460
You don't hire anybody
who isn't honest.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:34.000
And then after that,
let them do their work

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.310
and help them when you need to.

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.130
Once in a while tell
them, no but not often.

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:41.210
So I told them that.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:44.020
And then he said
to me, he said, OK.

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:50.830
At the end of that conversation,
I was about to leave, he said,

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:55.000
would you suggest some
things that I should say?

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.390
And I said, Rush, you
haven't been listening to me.

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:00.760
And he said, how not?

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.630
And I said, are you
the best at this?

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.500
And he said, yeah.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.540
I said, haven't I just
told you what I hope

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.910
you can help people understand?

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:12.850
He said, yeah.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:16.190
I said, probably you should
figure out how to do that, huh?

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:22.320
He's a very interesting guy.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.210
He worked.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:26.607
He was rich as Croesus.

00:12:26.607 --> 00:12:28.440
I don't know what he's
doing with the money.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:29.482
I hope he gives it to us.

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:34.260
But he has a family.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.810
And he grew up 50 miles
from where I grew up.

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.165
We played on the same baseball
field and little league

00:12:39.165 --> 00:12:40.080
a couple times.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:42.780
Not at the same time.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:47.980
And he didn't have to work.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:50.540
He just loved to work.

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:56.410
And he worked probably
12 hours a day,

00:12:56.410 --> 00:13:00.490
three doing the show,
nine getting ready.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:06.370
And he studied, and he would
text me and ask me questions

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.450
about things going on.

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:11.080
Anyway, he was a great man.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:14.120
And in this day
and age, of course,

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:19.420
he's been being painted in the
darkest colors in the press.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:23.600
Another thing is
Rush is hilarious.

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:25.040
He could be really serious.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.837
But then mostly, it
was just hilarious.

00:13:29.837 --> 00:13:31.420
I didn't listen to
his show very much.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.462
I talked to him more than
I listened to his show.

00:13:33.462 --> 00:13:34.930
But I listened sometimes.

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:38.170
Once it became a famous one.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:41.350
He was talking about his
life in his apartment

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:45.540
complex in New York before
he moved to Palm Beach.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:50.540
He said he'd go there,
and he'd get settled down

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:51.960
for a long evening's work.

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:55.400
And he'd order takeout
from, like, five places.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:58.550
He lost a lot of weight
before he died, I mean,

00:13:58.550 --> 00:13:59.750
years before he died.

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:01.880
But he was kind of tubby.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:06.050
And he said that it
became very inconvenient

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:11.510
because the takeout guys were
forbidden to come upstairs

00:14:11.510 --> 00:14:13.490
because they would go
all over the apartment

00:14:13.490 --> 00:14:16.400
complex or the condo
complex and put menus

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:21.200
under everybody's door, and
people thought that was tacky.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:22.800
Rush didn't mind.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.500
So it's causing him--

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:27.080
because he says, you know,
I'm in my boxer shorts,

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.720
and I got to put on my trousers
and go down and get my food.

00:14:29.720 --> 00:14:32.120
That's just terrible.

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:33.820
Until one day I figured it out.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:40.840
He called the-- what do
you call it, the porter?

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:45.945
He said, would you please
admit my dinner guest, Raoul?

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:58.770
I liked Rush.

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:00.580
I can't even remember
what it is now.

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.770
And of course, that's because
I'm a racist or something.

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:08.820
But he did something that
brought a storm down on him.

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:11.970
And I can't remember
what it was.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:15.900
But of course, Media
Matters is an outfit

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:21.180
that hassles conservatives
in the media,

00:15:21.180 --> 00:15:24.270
and they managed to
break into our server,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:26.910
and get the email
addresses of the students

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:29.160
and the faculty and the
staff, and send everybody

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.230
an email about how
shameful it was

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:35.370
that Hillsdale College would
advertise on Rush Limbaugh.

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:39.060
And that's a little
inconvenient.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:42.840
And it didn't cause much
trouble, oddly enough.

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:45.060
But then I got a call
from a journalist

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.340
from The Huffington Post.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.260
And I took it, and I
said, what do you want?

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:53.280
And she said, are you going
to cancel your advertisements

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:54.150
on Rush Limbaugh?

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:56.860
I said no, I'm not
going to do that.

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:57.780
And she said, why not?

00:15:57.780 --> 00:16:00.118
I said, because
you're the one asking.

00:16:00.118 --> 00:16:01.555
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.650
One of the most serious
things he ever said.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:12.180
He learned of that.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:15.320
And he lost a couple
of advertisers

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:18.270
over that, window
blinds or something.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:23.990
And he looked at me and he
said, that was very noble.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:25.010
And I said, why?

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:29.460
And he said, you
didn't run away.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.600
And I said, you
know, Rush, we were

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.180
the kind of people to do
that, would we have been here

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.370
in the first place?

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:41.240
It's a crazy thing, but
it was a good thing to do,

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:46.460
because how it works, we think
we have some beautiful things

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:47.960
to teach.

00:16:47.960 --> 00:16:49.550
We think they're world-saving.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.550
We think there's
soul-saving, whether they

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:54.870
serve the world or not.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.380
And we want to teach as
many people as we can.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.720
That's in the original articles.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:04.520
All who wish, without regard to
race, sex, or national origin.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:07.130
And that's what the college
was in the Civil War days

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:09.109
when it was a very
influential thing,

00:17:09.109 --> 00:17:11.099
and it's an influential
thing again.

00:17:11.099 --> 00:17:13.849
And so we want to talk
to a lot of people.

00:17:13.849 --> 00:17:16.819
And we don't advertise
on The Huffington Post

00:17:16.819 --> 00:17:22.260
because CNN is not
a good field for us.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:22.760
Why?

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:26.990
Because it's not so much just
that people are conservative.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:28.310
That helps.

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.020
It's that they're
conservative people who

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:34.080
are interested to
know serious things.

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:37.040
And so because of Rush
Limbaugh, but because of all

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.640
of the other things we do,
I'm proud to have before me

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:42.320
the best audience in America.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:43.760
Thank you.

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:50.200
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:50.740
OK.

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:54.100
I'm back on my Kathy
Grammer task now.

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:55.750
Raheem helped with that.

00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:57.110
You feel better, Kathy?

00:17:57.110 --> 00:17:58.600
Yeah, yeah.

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:01.300
Well, I have to
say some bad stuff,

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:04.210
and then you'll feel
better after that.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:08.635
Kathy likes for me
to manage her mood.

00:18:12.787 --> 00:18:14.620
By the way, I don't
think your grandchildren

00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:15.620
are as handsome as mine.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:17.830
What do you think?

00:18:17.830 --> 00:18:18.440
Wrong.

00:18:18.440 --> 00:18:18.948
Wrong.

00:18:18.948 --> 00:18:20.740
We're not going to
reach agreement on that.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:26.250
So here's the bad stuff.

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:28.920
What has happened
is revolutionary,

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.920
and why it has happened
is revolutionary

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:35.300
and exceedingly dangerous.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:39.200
The what is the electoral
system of the country

00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:41.420
is, at least in
part, compromised.

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:49.130
And the electoral system is
the fulcrum of everything.

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:52.850
It's a simple relationship.

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:56.360
We are the sovereign, and we
are outside the government.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.580
And the government has
all the power to act.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:18:59.930
We don't do anything ourselves.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:01.070
We're not the legislature.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:02.090
We're not the executive.

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:03.650
We don't judge.

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:06.000
We elect people who do it.

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.810
And that means when those
arrangements were set up,

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:10.880
they were sublimely effective.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:13.310
And Madison describes, in
another beautiful place

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:15.290
in The Federalist
Papers, the effect

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:20.060
of that because every
human use of power

00:19:20.060 --> 00:19:22.760
should never be
absolute or unchecked.

00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:26.740
And that includes even
by us, the sovereign.

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.320
So we can do anything
we want to do,

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.510
but it's going to
take some time.

00:19:31.510 --> 00:19:34.750
And that means we have
to think before we act.

00:19:34.750 --> 00:19:37.660
In that mood, James
Madison is like your mom.

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:39.910
Think before you act.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:42.940
All the rules, by the way,
that are worth making,

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:45.190
they're all stuff your mom
and dad would have told you

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:46.420
when you were a kid.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:51.370
And any other rules can
never be remembered.

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:53.560
So that's the thing.

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.210
The first step in
the Constitution

00:19:55.210 --> 00:20:01.820
is it delays our direct
control of the government.

00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:04.780
And it's not that way in
Athens, where the people

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:06.760
got in an amphitheater
and voted,

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:09.250
and then next week
got in an amphitheater

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.620
and voted something else.

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:13.660
And it's not like
the monarchies,

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:17.710
where the king or queen
is the executive branch.

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:24.710
We're just the sovereign, and
the government works for us.

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:27.230
But it does all the work.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:29.090
Now when those
relationships were set up,

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:33.110
they were amazingly stable for
a long time until the Civil War.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:37.100
And then they were restored by
the very great Abraham Lincoln.

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:39.470
But part of the reason for
that was the government

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:41.870
was about 10% of the
economic activity

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:43.760
in the country, 8% or 10%.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.370
And most of that was
federal and local.

00:20:46.370 --> 00:20:49.400
The federal government
was 1% or 2%.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:51.380
Well, now it's put
it all together,

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:53.450
and it's pretty much
a top down system now,

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:57.080
kind of unified, although
not fully unified,

00:20:57.080 --> 00:20:59.300
which is an important
strategic fact for the future.

00:21:03.160 --> 00:21:06.050
It's now over 50%.

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:10.240
So you wonder, why are all
these Fortune 500 companies so

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:12.820
politically correct?

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:14.680
Partly the cause
is their bosses all

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:19.030
went to the best colleges,
by which I mean the worst.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:21.520
And part of it is
explained by the fact

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:24.340
that the force that
can stand up to them,

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:27.100
if there is any in the
world, is the government,

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:31.410
and the government is staffed
now by a major bureaucracy.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.160
The actual work of the
American government

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:35.890
is done by, at the
federal level, about two

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:38.020
and 1/2 million
civilian employees,

00:21:38.020 --> 00:21:41.470
at the state level,
20 some million.

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:45.520
But they all follow rules that
are made inside the agencies,

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:48.640
not laws anymore
made in the Congress.

00:21:48.640 --> 00:21:51.070
So the point is
there's this system,

00:21:51.070 --> 00:21:55.120
and because it's so big,
it's a mighty weight

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:57.250
in the election process.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:00.280
And that's been a creeping
problem, big problem

00:22:00.280 --> 00:22:03.130
and creeping bigger,
for decades now,

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:04.720
and I've been
railing against it.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:12.460
Well, now, HR1, first bill
Nancy Pelosi has put in,

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:15.310
is going to put
DC in the Senate,

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:18.520
and it's going to impose
a national system of vote

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.260
harvesting.

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:22.330
And that means this
stuff that happens,

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:25.180
where you don't know where
the ballots come from.

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:25.942
Lots of ballots.

00:22:25.942 --> 00:22:27.400
Nothing wrong with
lots of ballots.

00:22:27.400 --> 00:22:28.817
It's just something
wrong with not

00:22:28.817 --> 00:22:30.700
knowing where they come from.

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:35.260
And I don't know if
Donald Trump won or not.

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:37.450
I don't myself know.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:40.150
But I know how
Michigan was stolen

00:22:40.150 --> 00:22:44.290
because I have the misfortune
sometimes to live there.

00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:46.900
And that gets me to the cause.

00:22:46.900 --> 00:22:51.460
First of all-- and what
are the things to mess

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:52.570
with the electoral system?

00:22:52.570 --> 00:22:57.100
Ballot harvesting, DC
statehood, a new interpretation

00:22:57.100 --> 00:22:58.420
of voting rights.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:01.170
Voting rights, that's
the 15th Amendment.

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:04.390
What was passed was
everybody gets to vote.

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:06.580
Well, that's much
embellished now,

00:23:06.580 --> 00:23:11.150
so that districts are
arranged to figure out

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:16.340
who wins on race and other
identity group lines now.

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:24.730
And so you could get a kind of
a nationally politically correct

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:32.190
constituency, 19 or 20
million illegals about

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:37.410
to be legal, probably by
executive order, which

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:41.130
is an interesting
phenomenon because one

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:44.820
of the causes of the
Revolutionary War

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:48.330
and the causes against the
king in the Declaration

00:23:48.330 --> 00:23:51.090
of Independence is
that he had expanded

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:55.020
the territory of
Quebec southwards.

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:58.890
And he did that because he
gave Quebec a new constitution,

00:23:58.890 --> 00:24:00.910
and they didn't kick about it.

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:02.400
And it was a
constitution he liked,

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:03.750
where he could control it.

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:06.330
And then he moved some
American colonists

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:07.920
into that Canadian colony.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:13.470
In other words, now it's not
the people choosing the rulers,

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.670
it's the rulers
choosing the people.

00:24:16.670 --> 00:24:19.640
So that, too, that illegal
immigration stuff, that just

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:22.350
reverses the
relationship perfectly.

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:23.600
And I think that's its object.

00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:29.040
And then anybody
who tries to strike

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:31.800
that down, Supreme
Court is living now

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:34.500
under a threat of being packed.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:36.390
And that is exactly
like the threat

00:24:36.390 --> 00:24:39.570
that Franklin
Roosevelt made in 1936.

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.700
And he probably
would have done it

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:44.730
except the court changed its
mind about all his programs

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.580
and started authorizing them.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:52.020
So that means a massive
political force,

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.900
which amounts to a rearrangement
of the Constitutional authority

00:24:54.900 --> 00:25:01.000
and the direction that power
flows, out rather than in.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:06.730
That is far advanced and
maybe complete, I don't know.

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:10.150
I'll tell you what
happened in Michigan.

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:12.400
We know them.

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:17.260
And my general counsel,
Harris Gray, he's

00:25:17.260 --> 00:25:18.880
been spending time with them.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:20.890
And he gives them
advice for nothing.

00:25:20.890 --> 00:25:22.870
Doesn't act for them.

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:24.050
He's also given advice.

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:27.310
He just told me yesterday
the recalcitrant Spengler's

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:30.820
restaurant in
Jonesville, Michigan,

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:35.020
six miles from the campus,
has refused to close.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:37.490
And Bob has been
helping them out.

00:25:37.490 --> 00:25:39.550
And I told Bob, I said,
that's controversial.

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:43.670
Please do, it but try to
stay out of the paper.

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:46.840
And then the next
night Mr. Spengler

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:54.340
was on Fox News wearing a
Hillsdale College sweatshirt

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:56.020
and thanking Bob
Norton for the help.

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:03.700
Now here's the interesting thing
about presidential elections.

00:26:03.700 --> 00:26:10.510
To understand the Constitutional
system, it's two things.

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:13.540
It is a system of
concentrating power

00:26:13.540 --> 00:26:17.170
more than was possible under
the Articles of Confederation.

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:22.450
But also it's a elaborate
system of distributing power out

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:23.470
across the land.

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:26.050
So first of all, the
federal government

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:29.200
has a list of 17 things in
Article I, Section 8 that it's

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:30.430
supposed to be able to do.

00:26:30.430 --> 00:26:33.820
States are not
constrained that way.

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.370
They can do whatever is
in the police power, which

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:40.413
is an old common law power to
act for the general welfare.

00:26:40.413 --> 00:26:41.830
So the federal
government is meant

00:26:41.830 --> 00:26:46.480
to be about certain things,
and supreme only on those.

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:50.140
An interesting fact, by the
way, is that education is not

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:51.850
listed in those 17 things.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:27:03.570
So the way elections run
are radically decentralized.

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:05.940
So states control.

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.812
And they control
their own elections,

00:27:08.812 --> 00:27:10.770
except now there are
court cases and stuff that

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:13.070
got the federal government
intervening all the time.

00:27:13.070 --> 00:27:14.380
They control their own.

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:18.000
But then for the
Federal elections,

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.180
each house, the
Senate and the House,

00:27:21.180 --> 00:27:26.100
is named to be the judge of the
credentials of its own members.

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:28.292
And that means if a
state sends a rep,

00:27:28.292 --> 00:27:30.000
and they think he's
fraudulently elected,

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.250
they can eject him and
ask for another one.

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:34.950
They can't name the other one,
they can ask for another one.

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:38.160
That rarely happens,
but it does happen.

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:41.380
Presidential elections,
now that means, by the way,

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.230
that laws were passed.

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:45.270
They're old in
Michigan, and they're

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:47.730
similar to the way the
election laws work in America,

00:27:47.730 --> 00:27:50.070
and they're mostly pretty good.

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:51.750
But they were passed
by the legislature

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:53.580
and signed by the governor.

00:27:53.580 --> 00:27:57.930
And that means that only another
law passed by the legislature

00:27:57.930 --> 00:28:00.720
and signed by the governor
could overturn it,

00:28:00.720 --> 00:28:03.180
except this summer.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:05.070
The attorney general
and the Secretary

00:28:05.070 --> 00:28:09.060
of State and the governor
began to negotiate changes

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:11.360
in the election laws.

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:14.510
This is the basic
Michigan election laws,

00:28:14.510 --> 00:28:17.330
and they can't do that by
themselves, but they did.

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:19.820
And the legislature,
a Republican majority,

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:25.520
which increased in the last
election in both houses,

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:27.860
did nothing.

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:33.790
I urged them to.

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:35.740
Didn't.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:40.150
The presidential election
is a special case,

00:28:40.150 --> 00:28:42.550
and there's a big debate about
this in the Constitutional

00:28:42.550 --> 00:28:43.450
Convention.

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:45.850
And of all of the provisions
in the Constitution,

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:49.480
this is one of the more clear
because what they wanted was

00:28:49.480 --> 00:28:54.190
they wanted to generalize
the process of selecting

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:57.860
the president and distribute
it all over the country.

00:28:57.860 --> 00:29:01.755
So the first idea was national
popular vote, James Madison

00:29:01.755 --> 00:29:03.130
and Governor
Clinton of New York.

00:29:03.130 --> 00:29:04.420
Strange bedfellows.

00:29:04.420 --> 00:29:07.990
They didn't get along
very much, mostly.

00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:11.060
That didn't go anywhere.

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:14.530
So then they said the governors
in the states should pick.

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:17.410
But the trouble is there
aren't very many governors.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:21.430
And so one could make
deals to get something

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:24.040
in exchange for his vote.

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:25.300
So they argued that through.

00:29:25.300 --> 00:29:29.890
And then the next stage
was state legislatures.

00:29:29.890 --> 00:29:32.470
They're numerous,
enough of them that it's

00:29:32.470 --> 00:29:33.775
hard to make individual deals.

00:29:38.170 --> 00:29:41.120
And then they added a
refinement on top of that.

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:44.320
And the refinement is exactly
parallel to the ratification

00:29:44.320 --> 00:29:48.550
process, where the Constitution
was ratified by conventions,

00:29:48.550 --> 00:29:50.110
by people elected.

00:29:50.110 --> 00:29:52.510
A ticket, yes or no,
to the Constitution.

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:54.370
And they serve that
one function and then

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:56.500
they disappeared
forever, which is

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:58.750
one of the reasons the
Constitution is not constantly

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:01.170
being amended.

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:04.230
Well, this, they said,
no, the legislators

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:09.810
shall be in control of the
manner of choosing electors.

00:30:09.810 --> 00:30:11.140
State legislatures.

00:30:11.140 --> 00:30:13.830
That means not the governor
and not the courts.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:18.820
State legislatures
have control of that.

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:21.640
And the only time the
federal government

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:25.960
can intervene in that is if
the states send conflicting

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:28.150
slates of legislators.

00:30:28.150 --> 00:30:29.650
And that did not
happen this time

00:30:29.650 --> 00:30:32.620
from any of the five
Republican-controlled

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:36.140
battleground states where
they accepted the results.

00:30:36.140 --> 00:30:39.850
However, in most of those
states- Michigan for sure,

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:43.030
also Pennsylvania,
also Wisconsin,

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:44.140
probably the others--

00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:46.000
there are two more--

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:49.150
they had watched their
procedures being changed

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:51.460
and not done anything about it.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:54.970
What we thought they
should do in the summer

00:30:54.970 --> 00:31:03.660
was announce that they have
sole control over the selection

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:05.250
of the electors.

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:06.210
And you're changing it.

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:09.590
We reserve the right
to pick our own slate.

00:31:09.590 --> 00:31:11.660
Now that would have
been a firestorm.

00:31:11.660 --> 00:31:13.952
All the national press.

00:31:13.952 --> 00:31:15.410
Somebody who runs
Google would have

00:31:15.410 --> 00:31:17.510
said something bad about them.

00:31:17.510 --> 00:31:20.570
And the NBC News
would be displeased.

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:24.050
In other words, their
life would be very intense

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:27.380
because they actually
have the power to do that.

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:31.800
And to do it, when
to do it, was August,

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.350
before the ballots
started coming in.

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:36.100
And they didn't.

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:41.700
And I know them, many of them.

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.140
I think I know why they didn't.

00:31:47.390 --> 00:31:51.670
It's something you learn if
you go to the right college.

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:56.650
What you are depends on
how you live your life.

00:31:56.650 --> 00:32:00.340
And they needed to do two
things that they didn't do.

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:03.970
And it's not just they
neglected two things.

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:06.670
These things would only come
if they lived their lives

00:32:06.670 --> 00:32:08.690
in a certain way.

00:32:08.690 --> 00:32:11.180
First of all, they
would need to know

00:32:11.180 --> 00:32:15.950
deeply their responsibilities
and authorities.

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:18.720
They take an oath
to pursue them.

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:23.580
On the 1776 Commission, I took
an oath to the Constitution.

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:25.890
First time I've ever done it.

00:32:25.890 --> 00:32:28.510
And I'm obliged to that now.

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:32.430
It doesn't end
because I got fired.

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:37.620
In the past, I was that.

00:32:37.620 --> 00:32:39.418
This alone is denied
even to God to make

00:32:39.418 --> 00:32:40.710
what has been not to have been.

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:47.610
And all this cancel
stuff, by the way.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:50.970
It is just the most obvious
truth in the world that that

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:53.500
cannot be done.

00:32:53.500 --> 00:32:58.170
And I fear it because
if they start doing it

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:02.040
to me in the various
ways they could, then I

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:04.980
would have a world of turmoil.

00:33:04.980 --> 00:33:07.530
But I wouldn't have the fear
that that could be ultimately

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:10.890
successful because it
happened, or it didn't.

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:15.600
They didn't live
their lives in a way

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:17.760
to understand deeply
things like the fact

00:33:17.760 --> 00:33:22.200
that they are in sole control
of the manner of choosing

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:23.190
the electors.

00:33:23.190 --> 00:33:25.470
What a tremendous
responsibility.

00:33:25.470 --> 00:33:28.320
What a tremendous power.

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:30.950
And they were unaware of it.

00:33:30.950 --> 00:33:32.990
One member of the
leadership didn't

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:37.130
know that the legislature
had the subpoena power.

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:41.650
Another one opined
that the governor was

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:44.980
the leader of the legislature.

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:46.540
And isn't that funny?

00:33:46.540 --> 00:33:48.730
Everybody here knows
better than that, right?

00:33:48.730 --> 00:33:50.560
And his argument
was, well, she has

00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:54.310
the veto power, which basically
gives her 1/3 of the votes.

00:33:54.310 --> 00:33:57.220
She's the senior legislator.

00:33:57.220 --> 00:34:01.120
And so there then ensued a
sophomore high school lesson

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:03.490
in separation of powers.

00:34:03.490 --> 00:34:09.730
And that person ran for
office, got elected,

00:34:09.730 --> 00:34:13.960
is a good person with broadly
correct political opinions,

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:16.120
according to my lights.

00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:20.650
He didn't know basic stuff.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:25.690
It's like a carpenter to
know how to build with wood.

00:34:25.690 --> 00:34:31.719
And the very best carpenters,
they live their lives in a way

00:34:31.719 --> 00:34:34.030
so they get really good at that.

00:34:34.030 --> 00:34:38.840
And it takes a commitment
more than the normal, you see,

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:41.520
and they haven't made that.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:48.090
And then the second thing is to
do bold and courageous things,

00:34:48.090 --> 00:34:50.480
you have to practice.

00:34:50.480 --> 00:34:51.920
You have to practice
in your mind.

00:34:55.219 --> 00:34:56.929
Another word for that is worry.

00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:06.500
Everybody who works for me
all, the vice presidents here,

00:35:06.500 --> 00:35:11.510
all the ones who are not here,
we basically live our jobs.

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:12.770
It's kind of weird.

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.410
And we like each other
and we love our jobs

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:17.420
and we know how to relax.

00:35:17.420 --> 00:35:21.030
But we practice all the time.

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:23.600
What if they do this,
what will we do?

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:26.210
What if we do that,
what will we do?

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:30.530
Every big step we
take is calculated

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:33.380
in light of its effects
upon the dangers that

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:35.640
might come upon the college.

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:39.830
And that's why the colleges
is the one independent one.

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:41.330
We care about it.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:42.800
We give ourselves to it.

00:35:42.800 --> 00:35:44.990
We study it all the time.

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:48.620
And then we think, how
will we comport ourselves

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:53.150
when terrible things
happen to us, as they will?

00:35:53.150 --> 00:35:55.460
And you have to get
your mind around that,

00:35:55.460 --> 00:35:59.390
and you have to get your
mind around that in advance.

00:35:59.390 --> 00:36:01.490
If you want to see
how that works,

00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:03.890
in Book II of
Aristotle's Ethics,

00:36:03.890 --> 00:36:07.310
there's a riveting
discussion of courage.

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:09.860
And in there he explains
what makes a courageous act.

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:11.210
And he says that if
you want to see them,

00:36:11.210 --> 00:36:12.835
look at the battlefield
because there's

00:36:12.835 --> 00:36:14.810
a lot of them happened
there, because everybody

00:36:14.810 --> 00:36:17.210
has good reason to be afraid.

00:36:17.210 --> 00:36:21.090
And the point is, if you do
that if you think about that,

00:36:21.090 --> 00:36:28.050
what would I do, then when it
comes, it's not new to you.

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:34.000
What would I do if the governor
and the Secretary of State

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:38.270
and the Attorney General set
out to alter the electoral laws,

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:40.870
including the ones
for the presidency,

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:43.270
without consulting us?

00:36:43.270 --> 00:36:48.850
What if doing that,
which they did,

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:51.720
they also refused to answer
any questions from us?

00:36:55.650 --> 00:37:00.340
In Michigan we have
two emergency laws.

00:37:00.340 --> 00:37:04.650
And I'm coming to think now
that we pass bad laws right

00:37:04.650 --> 00:37:06.330
after great wars.

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:09.990
One of them was passed
in 1946, and it basically

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:11.640
lets the governor
declare an emergency

00:37:11.640 --> 00:37:13.020
and sustain it for a long time.

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:13.610
Long.

00:37:13.610 --> 00:37:15.330
Doesn't say when it ends.

00:37:15.330 --> 00:37:22.170
And then in the '70s, they
passed a more sane emergency

00:37:22.170 --> 00:37:24.370
law, the more recent law, then.

00:37:24.370 --> 00:37:28.650
And in the '70s, it says that
she can declare an emergency.

00:37:28.650 --> 00:37:30.810
Within 30 days she's got
to go to the legislature

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:32.200
and get them to agree.

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.240
And then they can
extend for 30 days.

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:37.450
But every 30 days, she's
got to talk to them.

00:37:37.450 --> 00:37:39.450
That's the way they work
in all the good states.

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:40.920
And that's most of the states.

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:42.120
But no.

00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:45.240
She started out, by the
way, when the pandemic hit,

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:50.280
under the 1975 law, the one
where she had to consult.

00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:52.530
And then she got her
extension, and then

00:37:52.530 --> 00:37:54.120
they refused an extension.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:58.680
So she immediately went to
the 1945 law, which proves,

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:03.420
by the way, that she
knew about the 1975 law.

00:38:03.420 --> 00:38:09.370
And she just ran
over them for months.

00:38:09.370 --> 00:38:13.720
And they sue her in the courts.

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:17.060
What if, instead, they
just didn't vote any money?

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:20.200
She can't spend money without
them appropriating it.

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:25.030
They did the other day, in
an act of great boldness,

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:29.680
say that they're not going to
act on any of her appointments

00:38:29.680 --> 00:38:34.480
to commissions if she
doesn't straighten up.

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:40.490
And I happen to know,
as of two weeks ago,

00:38:40.490 --> 00:38:43.670
from election day
until two weeks

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:46.340
ago, the governor and
the Attorney General

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:48.230
and the Secretary
of State had not

00:38:48.230 --> 00:38:52.850
answered a single question from
the legislature, not in writing

00:38:52.850 --> 00:38:55.700
and not verbally.

00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:57.240
And they won't come and testify.

00:38:57.240 --> 00:39:01.210
They just flout them.

00:39:01.210 --> 00:39:05.860
Now what they should
have got, if they'd just

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:07.660
done this thing--

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:08.830
she couldn't stop that.

00:39:08.830 --> 00:39:11.260
The Constitution is
a very clever thing

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:13.210
if you know how it works.

00:39:13.210 --> 00:39:16.240
They could send a
second slate of electors

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:18.820
to Washington because
this slate wasn't

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:21.070
chosen according to the law.

00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:22.600
And there's no
power on Earth that

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:24.200
could stop them doing that.

00:39:24.200 --> 00:39:27.050
And all of the presidents
say that when that happens,

00:39:27.050 --> 00:39:31.150
then the Senate and the House
have got to make a decision.

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:32.630
And they didn't do that.

00:39:32.630 --> 00:39:34.090
They didn't threaten it.

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:36.250
And if they'd threatened
it-- or later done it,

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:38.440
but threaten it
would be way better--

00:39:38.440 --> 00:39:41.620
then they'd say, but
I'll tell you why.

00:39:41.620 --> 00:39:44.650
If we can devise some
procedures to make sure

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:48.760
that these ballots are held
in custody and protected,

00:39:48.760 --> 00:39:51.760
and that everybody gets
to vote, and everybody

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:56.410
gets to vote once, and
nobody gets to vote for them.

00:39:56.410 --> 00:40:00.160
And those requests would
have had wide public support.

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:03.398
They would have been
operating during a fire storm.

00:40:03.398 --> 00:40:06.065
But the only way you're going to
survive that and get any change

00:40:06.065 --> 00:40:10.440
is if you use something that
constitutes a real power.

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:14.100
And they have a real power,
and that's what it's for.

00:40:14.100 --> 00:40:16.530
And they don't have
an absolute power.

00:40:16.530 --> 00:40:17.490
That would be wrong.

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:20.010
And the governor doesn't
have an absolute power.

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:21.810
That would be wrong,
and wrong for her

00:40:21.810 --> 00:40:24.130
to pretend that she does.

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:26.990
So that means
that, first of all,

00:40:26.990 --> 00:40:30.970
it's very hard to know
it's to balance were fair.

00:40:30.970 --> 00:40:34.390
We know that there were
millions stored in warehouses.

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:38.210
The location of only one
of the warehouses is known.

00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:40.156
It was said once by
the Secretary of State

00:40:40.156 --> 00:40:44.290
that there was nine of them
scattered around the state.

00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:47.950
And the one that's known,
neighbors report people

00:40:47.950 --> 00:40:49.810
went in and out all the time.

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:52.360
What were they doing?

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:57.790
There weren't observers
as far as we know.

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:04.060
What that means is that's
a raw assertion of power

00:41:04.060 --> 00:41:05.215
over the people.

00:41:07.930 --> 00:41:12.640
And I must say that
was not pointed out

00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:16.630
by her opposition
in the legislature.

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:19.510
And that's what I say
is the worst thing.

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:26.323
I say that we didn't stop this
because we weren't ready to.

00:41:26.323 --> 00:41:28.490
And we weren't ready to
because we don't live right.

00:41:31.070 --> 00:41:34.320
But that also turns
to a good thing.

00:41:34.320 --> 00:41:39.230
And that is, isn't it true
that if you get slaughtered,

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:43.060
it's better if the
fault isn't you,

00:41:43.060 --> 00:41:46.020
because then maybe you
can do something about it.

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:47.020
How are we doing, Kathy?

00:41:47.020 --> 00:41:47.980
You feeling better?

00:41:47.980 --> 00:41:48.650
Yeah.

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:54.460
In other words, we don't
have to be Abraham Lincoln.

00:41:54.460 --> 00:41:57.910
We just have to get better
than these guys to do better.

00:41:57.910 --> 00:42:02.680
And so I actually
take heart from that,

00:42:02.680 --> 00:42:08.960
although this is a power
grab of the very first order.

00:42:08.960 --> 00:42:11.220
And where's it going to go?

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:14.570
I'll say my last gloomy thing.

00:42:14.570 --> 00:42:17.780
Did I tell you about this
Spanish professor I hired?

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:19.640
Did I talk about
that last night?

00:42:19.640 --> 00:42:22.070
It's my favorite
subject right now.

00:42:22.070 --> 00:42:23.780
This is a guy named
Carano, and he's

00:42:23.780 --> 00:42:27.230
going to teach Spanish
at Hillsdale College.

00:42:27.230 --> 00:42:30.560
And if you apply for a
job at Hillsdale College,

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:32.390
you won't be applying
for a better job

00:42:32.390 --> 00:42:35.180
because there isn't one because
it's got all the features.

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:37.490
Everybody's smart,
everybody works, everybody

00:42:37.490 --> 00:42:39.080
is nice to each other.

00:42:39.080 --> 00:42:44.150
The kids are naughty in
just the right amount,

00:42:44.150 --> 00:42:46.380
or sometimes a little more.

00:42:46.380 --> 00:42:54.600
But this guy was a full
professor in his 40s

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:59.470
at Venezuela, and a prominent
academic figure there.

00:42:59.470 --> 00:43:01.900
And he had to run for his life.

00:43:01.900 --> 00:43:04.650
He left his property behind.

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:07.620
He left his job behind.

00:43:07.620 --> 00:43:12.300
And he got to America because
they were inflating his salary

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:15.090
away and he lived
under constant threat

00:43:15.090 --> 00:43:17.845
about whatever he might say.

00:43:17.845 --> 00:43:19.470
And so now he's here
and he needs a job

00:43:19.470 --> 00:43:23.160
because he can't stay if he
doesn't get a really good job.

00:43:23.160 --> 00:43:26.460
It has to be a permanent job
to look right on the visa.

00:43:26.460 --> 00:43:30.560
So what it effectively
means is he needs this job.

00:43:30.560 --> 00:43:35.880
And there are two others
who are also very good.

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:37.640
And I drew all that out of him.

00:43:37.640 --> 00:43:38.870
I get him to tell me--

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:41.073
I try to find out
what do they love.

00:43:41.073 --> 00:43:42.740
You can always predict
people's behavior

00:43:42.740 --> 00:43:44.570
if you know what they love.

00:43:44.570 --> 00:43:49.560
And so got into all that,
and he told me all about it.

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:51.680
And of course, my
heart went out to him.

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:55.730
And I said, I don't know if
you're going to get this job

00:43:55.730 --> 00:43:57.060
or not.

00:43:57.060 --> 00:43:59.690
And I have the power to
give it to him by myself.

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:02.570
But I don't do that because
my colleagues are so good,

00:44:02.570 --> 00:44:05.040
and they have more time to
think about it than I do.

00:44:05.040 --> 00:44:07.320
And so I do overrule
them once in a while,

00:44:07.320 --> 00:44:10.670
but not often, and never
before I find out what

00:44:10.670 --> 00:44:12.347
they're going to do anyway.

00:44:12.347 --> 00:44:14.180
I said, I don't know
what's going to happen.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:17.120
But I'll make you a promise.

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:18.800
I've been running a
one-man immigration

00:44:18.800 --> 00:44:20.930
service for 40 years.

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:22.778
If I see somebody
really worthy, I

00:44:22.778 --> 00:44:25.070
try to help them become a
citizen of the United States.

00:44:25.070 --> 00:44:27.720
I will do that for you.

00:44:27.720 --> 00:44:31.490
And he cried in my office.

00:44:31.490 --> 00:44:37.510
And my point is that's what it's
like when your country sinks

00:44:37.510 --> 00:44:40.030
into despotism.

00:44:40.030 --> 00:44:44.000
It is not a happy thing.

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:46.800
So we have to stop that.

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:48.160
How?

00:44:48.160 --> 00:44:49.000
My last point.

00:44:52.130 --> 00:44:55.040
Come to find out I know a little
bit about Winston Churchill,

00:44:55.040 --> 00:45:00.080
and he is just the
man for right now.

00:45:00.080 --> 00:45:02.620
Look what happened
to him in his life.

00:45:02.620 --> 00:45:05.810
He spent his life foreseeing
and trying to prevent

00:45:05.810 --> 00:45:07.910
the great world wars.

00:45:07.910 --> 00:45:12.760
Instead, it was his
fate to fight them.

00:45:12.760 --> 00:45:16.810
He spent his life
warning about socialism.

00:45:16.810 --> 00:45:22.260
In the warnings he said,
it'll end the freedom

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:23.460
of the British people.

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:27.660
It will also end our
ability to feed ourselves.

00:45:27.660 --> 00:45:29.190
There'll be a collapse.

00:45:29.190 --> 00:45:32.250
We'll have to depopulate,
partly, the island.

00:45:32.250 --> 00:45:35.580
Those kind of warnings, all
his, life beginning in 1898

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:40.320
when he was 26 years
old, and continuing right

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:42.900
through to his death in '65.

00:45:42.900 --> 00:45:46.710
But in the 1945
election, they just

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:49.860
simply swept the socialists
for the first time.

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:54.610
They beat him, or his
party, the war hero.

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:55.740
And he was devastated.

00:45:55.740 --> 00:45:56.970
Do you see?

00:45:56.970 --> 00:46:01.050
He's in exactly our
position, except worse

00:46:01.050 --> 00:46:02.850
because I've been
warning about all this

00:46:02.850 --> 00:46:04.530
all my life,
warning that they're

00:46:04.530 --> 00:46:07.650
going to rig the electoral
system so you can't get them

00:46:07.650 --> 00:46:09.270
out.

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:12.180
And now it's happened, probably.

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:14.310
And I think about him.

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:17.340
And see, he had laid the ground.

00:46:17.340 --> 00:46:19.410
He was also-- how
old was he? '45.

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:26.680
He was 72.

00:46:26.680 --> 00:46:30.310
He'd won glory.

00:46:30.310 --> 00:46:33.010
Everybody thought he was the
greatest man in the world, even

00:46:33.010 --> 00:46:34.510
his enemies.

00:46:34.510 --> 00:46:38.170
And so of course, the
reliable Times of London,

00:46:38.170 --> 00:46:41.630
worse now, but bad
then, they called

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:43.330
it's time for him to retire.

00:46:43.330 --> 00:46:45.250
He can be the great
world statesman, which

00:46:45.250 --> 00:46:48.470
he did become, by the way.

00:46:48.470 --> 00:46:52.630
But now the Conservative
Party needs new blood.

00:46:52.630 --> 00:46:55.660
And the socialists are
going to get their chance.

00:46:55.660 --> 00:46:58.630
And he was devastated
by that election.

00:46:58.630 --> 00:47:02.480
Took him two, three weeks
to get his [? dauber ?] up.

00:47:02.480 --> 00:47:06.770
And then he says, I stay
till the pub closes,

00:47:06.770 --> 00:47:09.440
and I fight for my corner.

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:11.450
And then he launched
a whole strategy,

00:47:11.450 --> 00:47:14.450
and it's the one we
have to launch today.

00:47:14.450 --> 00:47:17.690
He said, we've been
warning about these things

00:47:17.690 --> 00:47:21.050
that they're going
to do for decades.

00:47:21.050 --> 00:47:24.140
Now they're going to do them.

00:47:24.140 --> 00:47:29.550
Now they will be
visible beyond mistake.

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:32.240
And so we are going
to oppose them.

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:34.760
We're going to call
out what they do.

00:47:34.760 --> 00:47:38.330
We're going to use that to
persuade the people that we

00:47:38.330 --> 00:47:40.430
have to change.

00:47:40.430 --> 00:47:45.050
Now it took seven years because
they won a huge majority

00:47:45.050 --> 00:47:46.280
and couldn't get him out.

00:47:46.280 --> 00:47:48.920
But he beat them, soon.

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:50.960
And then he gets to be
prime minister again.

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:52.880
And I'll give you
one of his arguments.

00:47:52.880 --> 00:47:55.700
This is the very best.

00:47:55.700 --> 00:47:57.480
And you know, it's
hard, by the way,

00:47:57.480 --> 00:48:01.460
to get arguments out these days
because social media and all

00:48:01.460 --> 00:48:02.820
that.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:05.660
But the truth is, the
alternatives are also

00:48:05.660 --> 00:48:08.150
easy and cheap.

00:48:08.150 --> 00:48:13.260
And they don't have a
monopoly on the internet.

00:48:13.260 --> 00:48:14.210
Here's a great thing.

00:48:14.210 --> 00:48:18.920
You know about
the browser Brave?

00:48:18.920 --> 00:48:20.600
Yeah.

00:48:20.600 --> 00:48:24.740
And they've done something
that I think is really awesome.

00:48:24.740 --> 00:48:26.360
There's a protocol.

00:48:26.360 --> 00:48:28.850
I'm about to be a techie
here for a minute.

00:48:28.850 --> 00:48:37.290
There's a protocol called
Interplanetary File System,

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:37.790
IPFS.

00:48:41.090 --> 00:48:44.600
And the guy who invented
it is from Stanford.

00:48:44.600 --> 00:48:45.890
He's a young guy.

00:48:45.890 --> 00:48:48.710
And you can find on YouTube
him giving a talk about it

00:48:48.710 --> 00:48:49.880
at Stanford.

00:48:49.880 --> 00:48:52.850
And this file system
is incorporated

00:48:52.850 --> 00:48:55.640
into the Brave browser now.

00:48:55.640 --> 00:48:57.670
Now here's what it does.

00:48:57.670 --> 00:49:02.630
The way the internet works is
to find any piece of information

00:49:02.630 --> 00:49:05.270
on it, you find the
domain where it is,

00:49:05.270 --> 00:49:08.270
and then you find the
thing in the domain.

00:49:08.270 --> 00:49:11.060
And it's the fact that
you get to all the files

00:49:11.060 --> 00:49:15.440
through a particular domain that
lets Facebook own everything

00:49:15.440 --> 00:49:19.930
on it, for example,
whereas is what this does

00:49:19.930 --> 00:49:25.960
is it replicates the file around
the internet in various places,

00:49:25.960 --> 00:49:30.520
and the file addresses
just to the file.

00:49:30.520 --> 00:49:33.530
And he says-- because
he's not stupid, this guy.

00:49:33.530 --> 00:49:34.220
He's very smart.

00:49:34.220 --> 00:49:36.460
He's a nerdy kind
of engineer guy.

00:49:36.460 --> 00:49:41.590
But what he says is that
will make the internet freer

00:49:41.590 --> 00:49:44.700
and harder to censor.

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:47.130
And it doesn't take
infrastructure.

00:49:47.130 --> 00:49:53.830
So right now, these
huge server farms

00:49:53.830 --> 00:49:58.360
that they deal with
half a continent, well,

00:49:58.360 --> 00:50:02.020
those belong to
about five companies.

00:50:02.020 --> 00:50:04.900
If you distribute this way, you
can just go through and around

00:50:04.900 --> 00:50:06.950
them both.

00:50:06.950 --> 00:50:10.520
Now it's an
interesting thing to me

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:13.868
because that guy gave
a very clever talk

00:50:13.868 --> 00:50:15.410
because he's dealing
with things that

00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:20.630
are very sensitive like
Google, the big gorilla,

00:50:20.630 --> 00:50:23.690
throwing off Parler and throwing
off Trump and throwing off

00:50:23.690 --> 00:50:25.150
everybody.

00:50:25.150 --> 00:50:27.305
And Amazon doing the
same, and all of them,

00:50:27.305 --> 00:50:30.050
and Apple doing the same.

00:50:30.050 --> 00:50:32.210
Those guys are bad.

00:50:32.210 --> 00:50:37.340
And he is giving to talk about
how you could get around that.

00:50:37.340 --> 00:50:38.630
And he does it very gently.

00:50:38.630 --> 00:50:40.520
I think he ought to
go on to politics.

00:50:40.520 --> 00:50:43.220
But you can tell
what he's saying.

00:50:43.220 --> 00:50:47.250
And then our commencement
speaker is Tim Allen this year.

00:50:47.250 --> 00:50:47.750
Yeah.

00:50:51.520 --> 00:50:57.040
I said to him, Mr. Allen, you're
the most requested commencement

00:50:57.040 --> 00:50:59.110
speaker after Clarence Thomas.

00:50:59.110 --> 00:51:01.030
And he said, Clarence Thomas?

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:02.260
I can kick his ass.

00:51:06.490 --> 00:51:09.370
Commencement is going
to be fun this year.

00:51:09.370 --> 00:51:11.590
But he says he knows a
bunch of people who are big

00:51:11.590 --> 00:51:13.810
up in the tech
world, and that they

00:51:13.810 --> 00:51:17.020
are very disturbed by
what's going on in there,

00:51:17.020 --> 00:51:20.550
and they want to
find a way out of it.

00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:21.720
Now that brings me.

00:51:21.720 --> 00:51:23.850
That's Churchill's point.

00:51:23.850 --> 00:51:29.040
His point is they're going
to do these things now,

00:51:29.040 --> 00:51:31.530
and they will be less able
to pretend that they're not

00:51:31.530 --> 00:51:33.700
what they seem.

00:51:33.700 --> 00:51:38.890
And people will be persuaded.

00:51:38.890 --> 00:51:39.910
Or else.

00:51:39.910 --> 00:51:46.450
By the way, the American people
are ready to be despotized,

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:49.980
and that means we're the
walking dead already.

00:51:49.980 --> 00:51:51.462
But I don't think that.

00:51:51.462 --> 00:51:53.045
And I'm just going
to close with this.

00:51:53.045 --> 00:51:57.660
This is one of the prettiest
things about Winston Churchill.

00:51:57.660 --> 00:52:02.670
I had a lifelong friend, Violet
Bonham Carter, beautiful woman,

00:52:02.670 --> 00:52:07.213
grandmother of Helena
Bonham Carter, the actress.

00:52:07.213 --> 00:52:09.380
And they were sweet on each
other for a little while

00:52:09.380 --> 00:52:11.270
when they were young.

00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:12.980
And it was before
Churchill figured out

00:52:12.980 --> 00:52:14.540
how to court a woman.

00:52:14.540 --> 00:52:18.590
So he would take her out and
they'd memorize noble speeches

00:52:18.590 --> 00:52:20.990
and poems.

00:52:20.990 --> 00:52:24.540
He got better when
Clemmy came along.

00:52:24.540 --> 00:52:28.500
But he memorized with Violet the
poem I'm going to read to you.

00:52:31.130 --> 00:52:36.710
And that was C 1904, 1903.

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:40.700
Got married in 1908.

00:52:40.700 --> 00:52:47.900
But now it's April 27,
1941, and Britain is alone.

00:52:47.900 --> 00:52:49.670
Russia and Germany are together.

00:52:49.670 --> 00:52:51.980
America is aloof.

00:52:51.980 --> 00:52:57.910
Overwhelming power being
brought to bear on the island.

00:52:57.910 --> 00:53:00.670
And two months later it
would change because Hitler

00:53:00.670 --> 00:53:03.590
attacked Stalin then.

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:08.540
That was a strategic
mistake he made, but left us

00:53:08.540 --> 00:53:11.600
with the Cold War afterwards.

00:53:11.600 --> 00:53:14.330
And so in this darkness-- it's
the lowest point right now--

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:21.170
he recites this
poem on the radio.

00:53:21.170 --> 00:53:23.660
This is by Arthur Hugh Clough.

00:53:23.660 --> 00:53:25.970
Say not the struggle
not availeth,

00:53:25.970 --> 00:53:28.460
the labor and the
wounds are vain,

00:53:28.460 --> 00:53:30.950
the enemy faints
not nor faileth,

00:53:30.950 --> 00:53:32.780
and as things have
been, they remain.

00:53:35.600 --> 00:53:39.410
If hopes were dupes,
fears maybe liars.

00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:41.990
It may be in yon
smoke concealed.

00:53:41.990 --> 00:53:46.310
Your comrades chase even now
the fliers, and but for you,

00:53:46.310 --> 00:53:49.030
possess the field.

00:53:49.030 --> 00:53:52.840
I'll interrupt to say
when you're in a fight,

00:53:52.840 --> 00:53:55.390
the pattern is always the
same at the beginning.

00:53:55.390 --> 00:53:57.280
There's a number of
the enemy, and then

00:53:57.280 --> 00:53:59.830
there's a larger number,
and there's a larger number.

00:53:59.830 --> 00:54:01.600
That's always how it starts.

00:54:01.600 --> 00:54:07.870
And of course, the mind,
full of fear, of course,

00:54:07.870 --> 00:54:10.950
imagines that the
number is infinite.

00:54:10.950 --> 00:54:15.750
But what this poem is about
is we don't know the number.

00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:17.880
We can only hope
and we must hope.

00:54:17.880 --> 00:54:23.190
He goes on, for while the
tired waves, vainly breaking,

00:54:23.190 --> 00:54:27.660
seem here no painful inch to
gain, far back, through creeks

00:54:27.660 --> 00:54:33.410
and inlets making, comes
silent, flooding in, the main.

00:54:33.410 --> 00:54:35.630
And not by eastern
windows only when

00:54:35.630 --> 00:54:38.810
daylight comes, comes
in the light, in front,

00:54:38.810 --> 00:54:41.840
the sun climbs slow, how slowly.

00:54:41.840 --> 00:54:45.900
But westward, look,
the land is bright.

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:48.330
That was an appeal
to us in the west.

00:54:51.090 --> 00:54:56.820
After he finished that, he
called Violet Bonham Carter.

00:54:56.820 --> 00:55:00.000
He didn't talk to her all
that much at that time.

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:03.830
And he was a very great man,
and she was an old woman.

00:55:03.830 --> 00:55:06.710
And he got her on the
phone and said, Violet?

00:55:06.710 --> 00:55:09.220
And she was bawling.

00:55:09.220 --> 00:55:13.700
And he said, did
you hear our poem?

00:55:13.700 --> 00:55:15.230
I'll close with this.

00:55:15.230 --> 00:55:17.620
That's our poem, too.

